(ns eve.guard
  "The automatic read guard — the no-guard seat supply (jank).

   eve.guard proper (eve/src/eve/guard.clj) is a JVM-only prototype: inside
   a guard extent (with-guard / guard*), an mmap atom's deref pins its
   domain once for the extent and answers its root as a LAZY slab-backed
   value behind a validity proxy (LazyMap / LazyColl), instead of
   materializing the whole root. bb, glojure and cljs have no guard at
   all — eve.atom's reader arms exclude them — because their deref always
   materializes (eve.atom/plain-deref), inside the epoch pin plain-deref
   itself takes: there is no lazy value for a hold to keep valid.

   Jank cannot take the reader exclusion. The seat's completion reads
   Eve's .cljc sources with :clj among the reader features
   (eve.host-completion's eve-load), so eve.atom takes its :clj arm and
   requires this namespace; but eve.guard exists upstream only as a .clj
   file and jank's loader has no .clj module type, so the completion's
   ns-form processing classified eve.guard clj-only and dropped the
   require clause, leaving every guard reference (guard/hold! first)
   unresolved at compile. This file is the seat's eve.guard: the guard's
   public surface with its extent-free semantics, which are the only
   semantics reachable here — no extent can open on this seat, because
   deref never produces a guarded lazy value (active? is false, so
   eve.atom's mmap-deref takes plain-deref, exactly as on bb/glj/cljs).

   Per var:
   - *extent*, current-cell: no extent exists; nil, the real guard's
     value outside an extent.
   - *proxy-same-domain?*: the real default, true; consulted only inside
     guard-deref, which active? gates off.
   - active?: false.
   - hold!: answers nil, the real hold!'s documented answer outside an
     extent. (Inside an extent it registers a pin; there are no extents
     here, and the pin a deref needs is plain-deref's own epoch pin.)
   - guard*, with-guard: run the body with no extent. Sound because the
     body's derefs materialize eagerly — which is precisely what the JVM
     contract demands of any value escaping an extent. This seat gains
     no laziness and loses no safety.
   - wrap, unwrap: identity. No validity proxy exists on this seat;
     wrap's only caller (eve.atom/guard-deref) is gated off by active?,
     and nothing here is an IGuarded.
   - valid?, check!: no cells exist; valid? is true (the real chain
     walk's base case at a nil cell) and check! does not throw.
   - materialize: the real semantics, seat-universal — d/eve->clj.
   Deliberately absent: the LazyMap / LazyColl validity proxies and the
   IGuarded protocol — JVM host machinery (clojure.lang deftypes) for
   values this seat never produces."
  (:require [eve.deftype-proto.data :as d]))

(def ^:dynamic *extent*
  "This thread's innermost guard extent. Always nil on this seat."
  nil)

(def ^:dynamic *proxy-same-domain?*
  "The real guard's default. Read only inside guard-deref, which
   active? gates off on this seat."
  true)

(defn active? [] false)

(defn current-cell [] nil)

(defn hold!
  "Outside an extent: nil (the real hold!'s documented answer). This
   seat is always outside an extent."
  [k acquire release]
  nil)

(defn guard*
  "Runs f with no guard extent (see the ns docstring for why that is
   sound on this seat). The receiving-domain arity ignores its first
   argument: there is no extent for a domain to own."
  ([f] (f))
  ([receiving-domain f] (f)))

(defmacro with-guard
  "A guard extent around body — on this seat, the body with no extent."
  [& body]
  (cons 'do body))

(defn valid? [c] true)

(defn check! [c] nil)

(defn wrap
  "x as the extent's lazy value — on this seat x itself: nothing is
   proxied here."
  [x cell]
  x)

(defn unwrap
  "The slab-backed value under a proxy — on this seat x itself:
   nothing is proxied here."
  [x]
  x)

(defn materialize
  "The one explicit materialization point: x as plain data."
  [x]
  (d/eve->clj x))
